{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OUTBOUND CELL — STILL FAILING, AND MY LAST TWO DIAGNOSES WERE BOTH WRONG. Recording precisely so\nthe next attempt starts from facts, not from my guesses.\n\nRUN: 30708665093, job 91392103475 — 25 passed, 1 failed, collected=26 executed=26 skipped=0.\nOnly `test_a_local_edit_reaches_the_dc_issue_outbound` fails:\n\n  rebar._errors.RebarError: rebar edit failed (exit 1):\n  Error: ticket system not initialized. Run 'ticket init' first.\n  (raised at src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:504)\n\nWHAT IS ESTABLISHED AND MUST NOT BE RE-TESTED:\n * The failure is at the LOCAL EDIT, before any outbound pass. The bridge is not implicated.\n * `bound_dc_issue` SUCCEEDS. It asserts `get_jira_key(local_id) == key` before yielding, and the\n   failure is after it — so the binding IS established over the scrubbed real store copy. The\n   fixture all 14 outbound UPDATE cells depend on works.\n * The other 25 cells pass, including both inbound legs (create PLANNED, and the ticket APPLIED)\n   and all three isolation layers.\n\nTWO WRONG HYPOTHESES, both mine, both now excluded:\n 1. \"REBAR_ROOT is unset so a subprocess resolves the ambient checkout.\" I set it (change 1168)\n    and the failure is UNCHANGED. Worse, the premise was false: `_python_leaf`\n    (`_lib_writes.py:483-504`) calls `fn(*args, repo_root=repo_root, **kwargs)` **IN-PROCESS**.\n    There is no subprocess. \"rebar edit failed (exit 1)\" is only the error FORMATTING — a\n    `CommandError` mapped onto `RebarError` to keep the exit-code contract. So `repo_root` was\n    being threaded correctly all along and REBAR_ROOT was irrelevant. The change is harmless and\n    can stay, but it fixed nothing.\n 2. \"`canonical_tracker` normalises the path somewhere unexpected.\" It does not — `lock.py:123-126`\n    is just `os.path.realpath`.\n\nWHAT IS NOT YET KNOWN — the next step, and DO NOT SKIP IT BY GUESSING AGAIN. The message\n\"ticket system not initialized\" occurs in THREE places and I never confirmed which one fired:\n    `_store/event_append.py:123`  (guard: tracker is a dir AND `tracker/.git` exists)\n    `_engine_support/reads_cli.py:329` and `:372`\n    `_cli/_init.py:131`\n`event_append`'s guard cannot obviously be the one, because the reconciler writes to this same\nstore successfully in the SAME test session — the inbound cell's ticket lands and the binding\npersists, which means `<work>/.tickets-tracker/.git` exists and is writable. So either a\nDIFFERENT guard fired, or the tracker path reaching it is not `<work>/.tickets-tracker`.\nGet the ANSWER before the next fix: add the resolved tracker path (and which guard) to the\nfailure output, or reproduce `rebar.edit_ticket(local_id, repo_root=<copy>)` locally against a\nhand-built copy — the fixture's construction is already reproducible without the harness (it was\nused to verify the git-repo fix), so this does not need a CI cycle to investigate.\n\nMETHOD NOTE, since this is the second time it has paid off: the one thing that reliably localised\na fault here was SPLITTING an ambiguous assertion into two cells (change 1163 found the filter bug\non its first run after four blind attempts). If the next attempt cannot pin the guard by reading,\nsplit this cell the same way — assert \"the local edit is recorded in the store\" separately from\n\"the edit surfaces on the DC issue\"."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785604010259926001,"uuid":"4e898f26-ea06-40ae-8114-ffb41f0a7394"}